WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.923
APIs often expose data and business operations directly to clients, services, partners, and automation.

2
00:00:06.923 --> 00:00:10.846
That makes API authorization a first-class design concern.

3
00:00:10.846 --> 00:00:25.077
Scopes and claims at a high level may describe requested permissions or facts about the subject, but the application still needs to validate whether the caller may perform the requested action on the requested resource.

4
00:00:25.077 --> 00:00:33.077
Delegated access means a user or system allows another application or service to act with limited permission on its behalf.

5
00:00:33.077 --> 00:00:37.924
Delegation should be narrow, visible, revocable, and governed by policy.

6
00:00:37.924 --> 00:00:43.154
A delegated token or permission should not silently become full account access.

7
00:00:43.154 --> 00:00:51.462
The system should preserve enough context to know who delegated access, which client is acting, and what actions are permitted.

8
00:00:51.462 --> 00:00:59.001
Service-to-service authorization and workload identity matter because not every request comes from a human user.

9
00:00:59.001 --> 00:01:08.232
Internal APIs, microservices, partner APIs, webhooks, background jobs, and automation often use service accounts or workload identities.

10
00:01:08.232 --> 00:01:11.847
Internal traffic should not automatically be trusted.

11
00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:18.155
A request from inside the network can still be mistaken, overprivileged, stale, or compromised.

12
00:01:18.155 --> 00:01:25.386
API gateways can centralize some controls, but they do not remove the need for application-layer enforcement.

13
00:01:25.386 --> 00:01:35.232
Gateways may validate tokens, rate limits, routes, or broad scopes, while the application still understands business objects and tenant boundaries.

14
00:01:35.232 --> 00:01:44.770
Authorization context must travel safely across service boundaries without being inflated, stripped, or trusted from unverified client input.
